Continuing Phase 2: Workflow Configuration in my GitHub Actions learning journey!

📌 What are Branch Filters?
Branch filters in GitHub Actions allow us to control which branches trigger a workflow.
Instead of running a workflow for every push or pull request, we can specify particular branches such as main, develop, or feature/*.
This helps avoid unnecessary workflow executions and keeps CI/CD pipelines organized.

🛠️ Practical Example:
name: Branch Filter
 Demo
  on:
   push:
     branches:
       - main
       - develop

  jobs:
   demo:
     runs-on: ubuntu-latest
     steps:
       - name: Display Message
         run: echo "Workflow triggered by a selected branch!"

🔍 How it works:
push: Triggers the workflow when code is pushed.
branches: Defines which branches should trigger the workflow.
main: Runs the workflow when changes are pushed to the main branch.
develop: Also runs the workflow when changes are pushed to the develop branch.
Other branches will not trigger this workflow through the configured push event.

💡 Key Takeaway:
Branch filters help control workflow execution based on specific branches, making CI/CD automation more efficient and manageable.
